text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":1}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Employers should move to a four-day workweek without reducing pay."}]]}],["$","p","1",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":2}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The strongest reason is retention. Replacing a trained employee costs an employer substantially more than keeping one, and surveys consistently find schedule flexibility ranking among the top reasons workers give for staying in a job or leaving it."}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":3}],["$","span",null,{"children":"A second reason is output. In several published trials, companies that shortened the week reported that productivity held steady or improved. Employees reported using the extra day for the errands, appointments, and family obligations that otherwise leak into working hours."}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":4}],["$","span",null,{"children":"My own former manager switched our team to four days and said afterward that she would never go back. Everyone on that team was happier."}]]}],["$","p","4",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":5}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Critics raise the obvious objection: some work cannot be compressed. A hospital ward, a bus route, and a restaurant kitchen all require coverage during fixed hours, and a shorter week for those workers means hiring more of them, not rearranging the same hours."}]]}]]}],"$undefined","$L28"]}]]}],"$L29"]}]]}]
1b:["$","section",null,{"id":"try-it","className":"scroll-mt-32 border-t border-eng-navy-200 pt-10","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-2 flex items-center gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-eng-navy-800 text-sm font-bold text-white","aria-hidden":"true","children":3}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-eng-teal-700","children":"Try It"}]]}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-2xl font-bold text-eng-navy-900 sm:text-3xl","children":"Your turn, with support"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 max-w-[68ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-600","children":"One question, with a hint available. Nothing here is graded."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":["$undefined",[["$","article","ev-guided-fees",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white shadow-sm","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-eng-navy-100 px-4 py-4 sm:px-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-2","children":[["$","h4",null,{"className":"font-display text-lg font-bold text-eng-navy-900","children":"Overdraft Fees"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-eng-teal-50 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-eng-teal-800","children":"Argument"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-xs text-eng-navy-400","children":[79," words"]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-4 py-5 sm:px-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-[68ch] space-y-4 text-[1.0625rem] leading-[1.75] text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":["$undefined",["$","span",null,{"children":"Overdraft fees should be capped by regulation. Banking data has repeatedly shown that a small share of account holders pay the large majority of all overdraft fees collected, and that this group is concentrated among customers with the lowest average balances. A fee designed as a deterrent has become a recurring charge falling on the customers least able to absorb it. My cousin once paid four overdraft fees in a single week, which shows how quickly they add up."}]]}]]}],"$undefined",["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-5 border-t border-eng-navy-100 pt-3 text-xs italic text-eng-navy-400","children":"Original passage — BEOC Academic Bridge"}]]}]]}]],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","$L2a","ev-g1",{"question":{"id":"ev-g1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which sentence provides the strongest support for the author's claim?","passageId":"ev-guided-fees","choices":["Overdraft fees should be capped by regulation.","Banking data has repeatedly shown that a small share of account holders pay the large majority of all overdraft fees collected.","A fee designed as a deterrent has become a recurring charge.","My cousin once paid four overdraft fees in a single week."],"correctAnswer":1,"hint":"One of these sentences is the claim itself, one is an interpretation, and one is a single story. Only one brings outside data.","explanation":"Support has to come from outside the claim. The banking data is the only sentence offering verifiable, repeated evidence about a pattern across many customers.","choiceRationales":["This is the claim. A claim cannot support itself.","Correct. Repeated data about a population is relevant, sufficient, and representative.","An interpretation of the data rather than evidence in its own right.","A single anecdote. It illustrates the problem vividly but proves nothing about the pattern."],"skill":"evidence","difficulty":"Developing"},"number":1}]]}]]}]]}]
1c:["$","section",null,{"id":"practice","className":"scroll-mt-32 border-t border-eng-navy-200 pt-10","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-2 flex items-center gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-eng-navy-800 text-sm font-bold text-white","aria-hidden":"true","children":4}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-eng-teal-700","children":"Practice"}]]}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-2xl font-bold text-eng-navy-900 sm:text-3xl","children":"Practice on a full passage"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 max-w-[68ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-600","children":"A longer passage with several questions. Check each answer as you go."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":["$undefined",[["$","article","ev-practice-transit",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white shadow-sm","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-eng-navy-100 px-4 py-4 sm:px-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-2","children":[["$","h4",null,{"className":"font-display text-lg font-bold text-eng-navy-900","children":"Free Fares"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-eng-teal-50 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-eng-teal-800","children":"Argument"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-xs text-eng-navy-400","children":[175," words"]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-4 py-5 sm:px-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-[68ch] space-y-4 text-[1.0625rem] leading-[1.75] text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":1}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The city should eliminate fares on its bus system."}]]}],["$","p","1",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":2}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Fare collection is expensive in ways that are easy to overlook. Fare boxes must be maintained, revenue must be counted and transported, and fare enforcement requires staff. In some smaller systems, these costs consume a meaningful share of the revenue the fares bring in."}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":3}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Free fares also speed up service. Boarding is the slowest part of a bus route, and passengers who do not need to pay board through any door. One transit agency that removed fares on a downtown route reported that average trip times fell by roughly nine percent."}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":4}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Ridership rises as well. Every system that has tried free fares has seen ridership grow, in some cases dramatically."}]]}],["$","p","4",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":5}],["$","span",null,{"children":"A friend of mine stopped driving entirely after her city made buses free, which shows that people will change their habits when the barrier is removed."}]]}],["$","p","5",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":6}],"$L2b"]}]]}],"$undefined","$L2c"]}]]}]],"$L2d"]}]]}]
1d:["$","section",null,{"id":"challenge","className":"scroll-mt-32 border-t border-eng-navy-200 pt-10","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-2 flex items-center gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-eng-navy-800 text-sm font-bold text-white","aria-hidden":"true","children":5}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-eng-teal-700","children":"Challenge"}]]}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-2xl font-bold text-eng-navy-900 sm:text-3xl","children":"A harder, TABE-style set"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 max-w-[68ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-600","children":"Denser text and less obvious answers — closer to what the test will ask of you."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"max-w-[68ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":"Every paragraph after the first offers support, but the support is not equally strong. For each question, restate the exact claim being supported before you look at the choices — several choices are true sentences that support a different point."}],[["$","article","ev-challenge-lots",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white shadow-sm","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-eng-navy-100 px-4 py-4 sm:px-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-2","children":[["$","h4",null,{"className":"font-display text-lg font-bold text-eng-navy-900","children":"Put the Empty Lots to Work"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-eng-teal-50 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-eng-teal-800","children":"Argument"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-xs text-eng-navy-400","children":[346," words"]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-4 py-5 sm:px-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-[68ch] space-y-4 text-[1.0625rem] leading-[1.75] text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":1}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Most older neighborhoods have one: a vacant lot behind a chain-link fence, owned by the city because the last owner stopped paying taxes. Some have sat empty for a decade. The city should lease these lots to neighborhood groups as community gardens, starting with every lot that has gone unsold for more than two years."}]]}],["$","p","1",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":2}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The first reason is money. An empty lot is not free to own: the city pays crews to mow it, fix its fence, and haul away dumped mattresses and tires. Two years ago, a city pilot let volunteer groups plant gardens on twelve lots. According to the pilot's first-year report, crews were sent to those twelve lots 140 times in the year before the gardens opened and 9 times in the year after, because the gardeners now handle the mowing and cleanup themselves."}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-right text-sm font-semibold tabular-nums text-eng-navy-300","aria-hidden":"true","children":3}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Gardens also put fresh food on tables that need it. Loretta Banks, a home health aide with a pilot plot, grows enough tomatoes, peppers, and greens that she has not bought a vegetable from June through September. Multiply her summer across every vacant lot, and grocery bills would fall across the city."}]]}],"$L2e","$L2f"]}],"$undefined","$L30"]}]]}]],"$L31"]}]]}]
