ost-of-collection reason, not the speed reason."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"One transit agency that removed fares on a downtown route reported that average trip times fell by roughly nine percent."}]," ","— ","Correct. A measured outcome on the exact variable the claim concerns."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every system that has tried free fares has seen ridership grow."}]," ","— ","Ridership is a different outcome. More riders could in principle slow boarding, not speed it."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"A friend stopped driving entirely after her city made buses free."}]," ","— ","An anecdote about one person's driving, unrelated to trip times."]}]]}]]]}]]}]
34:["$","details","ev-p3",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",3]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"Which paragraph contains the WEAKEST support for the author's claim, and why?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"C. Paragraph 5, because a single friend's experience is treated as proof of a general pattern"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"Paragraph 5 is the clearest case of anecdote standing in for evidence: one person's behavior, presented with \"which shows,\" as though it established a general rule. Paragraph 4 is also weak — an unsupported \"every system\" claim — but paragraph 5 explicitly asks a single story to do the work of data."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Paragraph 2, because the costs of fare collection are hard to measure"}]," ","— ","Paragraph 2 names specific, checkable costs. It is among the better-supported paragraphs."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Paragraph 4, because it makes a sweeping claim about every system with no evidence attached"}]," ","— ","A genuine weakness — \"every system\" is a sweeping claim — but paragraph 5 is the weaker of the two, because it presents one anecdote as proof."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Paragraph 5, because a single friend's experience is treated as proof of a general pattern"}]," ","— ","Correct. \"Which shows\" asks one friend's experience to establish a pattern it cannot establish."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Paragraph 6, because it undermines the author's own argument"}]," ","— ","Paragraph 6 acknowledges a limitation honestly. Naming an unanswered objection is a strength in an argument, not weak support."]}]]}]]]}]]}]
35:["$","details","ev-p4",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",4]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"The author admits in paragraph 6 that the funding question is unanswered. In two or three sentences, explain what evidence the author would need to add to make the argument sufficient."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-teal-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-teal-800","children":["Model response:"," "]}],"The author would need evidence about how the lost fare revenue would be replaced — for example, figures showing what share of the operating budget fares actually cover, and a specific funding source that could cover the gap. Evidence from a comparable city that eliminated fares and sustained service afterward would also strengthen the case. Without that, the argument establishes benefits but never shows that the plan is affordable."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"A sufficient argument has to address the strongest objection to it. Here that means showing where replacement revenue comes from, with figures rather than assurances."]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]
36:["$","section","ev-challenge",{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-display text-lg font-bold text-eng-navy-900","children":"Challenge"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","details","ev-c1",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",1]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"What is the author's central claim?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"B. The city should lease its long-unsold vacant lots to neighborhood groups as community gardens."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"The last sentence of paragraph 1 states the position: \"The city should lease these lots to neighborhood groups as community gardens, starting with every lot that has gone unsold for more than two years.\" Paragraphs 2 through 4 each give a reason for it, paragraph 5 answers an objection to it, and the final sentence restates it."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Vacant lots cost the city money to mow, fence, and clean up."}]," ","— ","A reason from paragraph 2. It explains why the author wants the change; it is not the change itself."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"The city should lease its long-unsold vacant lots to neighborhood groups as community gardens."}]," ","— ","Correct. It is the position that every other paragraph either supports or defends."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Community gardens can end grocery bills for the families who work them."}]," ","— ","An overstated version of the food reason in paragraph 3. It is support the author offers, not the position that support is for."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"The city should hold its vacant lots for developers who will build housing."}]," ","— ","This is the objection the author answers in paragraph 5 — the opposite of the author's position."]}]]}]]]}]]}],["$","details","ev-c2",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":["$L40","$L41"]}],"$L42","$L43","$L44"]}]]}]
37:["$","$L2a","ev-m2",{"question":{"id":"ev-m2","type":"evidence-selection","question":"Which sentence from the passage is evidence, rather than the claim or a restatement of it?","passageId":"ev-mastery-vans","choices":["Cameras are the right investment for this fleet, and the sooner we add them, the sooner we stop paying for the same accident again and again.","Backing up is the riskiest part of a driver's shift.","The company should install backup cameras in every delivery van.","The safety office's accident log shows that 15 of the 22 van accidents drivers reported last year happened while backing up."],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"Evidence answers the question of how the author knows. Only the accident log names a source and gives a count you could check. The first and last sentences state the claim, and the sentence about risk is a reason that the log then proves. If you missed this, revisit \"Claim, reason, evidence\" — evidence is the specific material behind a reason, not another way of saying the claim.","choiceRationales":["A restatement of the claim in the last sentence. It sounds like a conclusion, but it adds no new support.","A reason, not evidence. It tells you why to accept the claim; the accident log is what shows the author knows it is true.","This is the claim itself. A claim cannot be its own evidence.","Correct. It names a source and gives specific numbers."],"skill":"evidence","difficulty":"Developing"},"number":2}]
38:["$","$L2a","ev-m3",{"question":{"id":"ev-m3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"The author includes the average repair cost and the price of a camera mainly to —","passageId":"ev-mastery-vans","choices":["show that drivers have been careless with company vans","show that preventing even a few backing accidents would more than pay for the cameras","prove that cameras will prevent every backing accident","explain why drivers often have no one outside to guide them"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The two prices turn the claim into a money decision: one $2,100 repair costs as much as seven cameras. The detail supports the cost side of the argument, not the safety side. If you missed this, revisit \"Claim, reason, evidence\" — each piece of evidence holds up a specific reason, and this one holds up the reason that cameras are worth what they cost.","choiceRationales":["Not in the passage. The author never blames the drivers for the accidents.","Correct. At these prices, a single prevented repair covers several cameras.","Too strong. The prices show what an accident costs; nothing in the passage shows that cameras prevent every accident, or any particular number of them.","The prices have nothing to do with guiding drivers. That detail belongs to the reason about risk."],"skill":"evidence","difficulty":"TABE Ready"},"number":3}]
39:["$","$L2a","ev-m4",{"question":{"id":"ev-m4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which additional evidence would most strengthen the author's argument?","passageId":"ev-mastery-vans","choices":["A driver's account of backing a van into a loading dock","A list of the camera models the parts supplier sells, with their features","Accident records from vans that already have cameras, showing whether backing accidents fell","A count of how many times each driver backs up during a typical shift"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"The passage proves that backing accidents are common and expensive, but it never shows that cameras reduce them — and the whole cost comparison depends on that link. Evidence from vans that already have cameras would fill the gap directly. If you missed this, revisit \"Three tests for evidence\": the evidence here is relevant, but it is not yet sufficient.","choiceRationales":["A single story is anecdote, and it would only add to the proof that backing accidents happen — which the accident log already shows.","Product features do not show whether cameras reduce accidents. This evidence is not relevant to the gap in the argument.","Correct. It tests the link the argument assumes but never proves: that cameras actually prevent backing accidents.","This would add detail to a reason that is already supported. The log shows that backing is where accidents happen; the missing piece is whether cameras help."],"skill":"evidence","difficulty":"TABE Ready"},"number":4}]
3c:["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-green-700","children":"The answer"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":"The output reason in paragraph 3 is best supported, because it rests on several published trials and offers a mechanism. The personal example in paragraph 4 is weakest, because a single team treated as proof is anecdote rather than evidence. The unanswered objection in paragraph 5 also limits the claim, since it identifies workplaces the argument does not address."}]]}]
3d:["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-eng-gold-500 bg-eng-gold-50 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-gold-700","children":"Take this with you"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":"The most memorable paragraph is often the weakest one. Vivid detail is not the same as sufficient evidence."}]]}]
3e:["$","$L2a","ev-c4",{"question":{"id":"ev-c4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which detail supports a claim slightly different from the one the author uses it to support?","passageId":"ev-challenge-lots","choices":["The pilot report's count of crew visits to the twelve lots before and after the gardens opened","Loretta Banks's summer harvest from her pilot plot","The builder who bought one of the pilot lots last spring","The county survey of residents who live near a public park"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"Restate the claim in paragraph 4 first: gardens bring neighbors together. The survey is about parks, not gardens, so at most it supports a claim about parks — and even then it shows only that living near a park and trusting neighbors go together, not that one causes the other. The author closes the gap with an assumption, that a garden \"would do the same work,\" rather than with evidence.","choiceRationales":["This supports exactly the point it is used for — that gardens save the city money. It is relevant, measured, and sourced.","Weak support, but for the right claim: it really is about a garden producing food. Its problem is sufficiency — one gardener cannot show what would happen across the city — not relevance.","This supports exactly the point it is used for — that a garden does not block a sale.","Correct. The survey measures something about parks, and the author stretches it to cover gardens. It supports a nearby claim, about parks, that the author did not make."],"skill":"evidence","difficulty":"Challenge"},"number":4}]
3f:["$","$L2a","ev-c5",{"question":{"id":"ev-c5","type":"short-response","question":"Is the author's evidence sufficient to support the central claim? In three or four sentences, name the strongest support in the passage, name one reason whose support falls short and explain what is wrong with it, and give your overall judgment.","passageId":"ev-challenge-lots","explanation":"A complete answer does three jobs: it credits the support that holds up, names the specific weakness in the support that does not — an anecdote standing in for evidence, or evidence about a different claim — and reaches a judgment that fits the evidence. The strongest answers notice that the evidence supports a narrower claim than the one the author makes. Judging the evidence insufficient is not the same as calling the claim false; the question is only whether the author supported it.","sampleResponse":"The strongest support is the pilot's first-year report, which found that crew visits to the twelve garden lots fell from 140 to 9 in a year, and the author also answers the developer objection with the sixty-day lease term and a lot that was actually sold. The other two reasons fall short: the food reason rests on one gardener's summer, and the neighbor reason rests on a survey about parks rather than gardens. Even the best evidence covers only twelve lots over a single year. The evidence is sufficient for a narrower claim, that gardens save the city money without blocking sales, but not for the author's promise that gardens would lower grocery bills and build trust across the city.","skill":"evidence","difficulty":"Challenge"},"number":5}]
40:["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",2]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"Which sentence from the passage best supports the answer to the previous question?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}]
41:["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"C. According to the pilot's first-year report, crews were sent to those twelve lots 140 times in the year before the gardens opened and 9 times in the year after, because the gardeners now handle the mowing and cleanup themselves."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"Restate the answer you gave to the previous question: the city should turn its long-unsold lots into gardens. The best support is evidence about gardens on the city's own lots, with a named source and numbers you could check. The pilot report's before-and-after count of crew visits is the only choice that does all three, and it shows the change the author wants already working on twelve lots."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Loretta Banks, a home health aide with a pilot plot, grows enough tomatoes, peppers, and greens that she has not bought a vegetable from June through September."}]," ","— ","The most vivid sentence in the passage, and that is the trap. It is one gardener's summer — an anecdote standing in for evidence — and it cannot show what gardens would do across the city."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Some council members object that the lots should be kept ready for developers, since new housing would bring in property taxes that a garden never will."}]," ","— ","This sentence states the objection. It supports the opposite position, holding the lots for developers, not the author's claim."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"According to the pilot's first-year report, crews were sent to those twelve lots 140 times in the year before the gardens opened and 9 times in the year after, because the gardeners now handle the mowing and cleanup themselves."}]," ","— ","Correct. It names a source, gives before-and-after numbers, and describes gardens on the city's own lots, so it bears directly on the claim."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Until then, the city should let its neighbors put the empty lots to work."}]," ","— ","This restates the claim in new words. A claim cannot support itself."]}]]}]]]}]
42:["$","details","ev-c3",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",3]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"In paragraph 5, the author argues that a garden would not stop the city from selling a lot. Which TWO details provide the strongest support for that point? Select two."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"A. \"Every pilot lease lets the city end it with sixty days' notice.\" (paragraph 5)  •  D. \"When a builder bought one of the twelve lots last spring, the garden group harvested its last crop and cleared the lot before the deadline.\" (paragraph 5)"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"Restate the point first: a garden would not get in the way of a sale. Two details bear on exactly that — the lease term that lets the city take a lot back, and the one time a pilot lot was actually sold. The crew-visit numbers are the strongest evidence in the whole passage, but they support a different point, which is why they are the most tempting wrong answer."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"\"Every pilot lease lets the city end it with sixty days' notice.\" (paragraph 5)"}]," ","— ","Correct. It shows the city keeps the power to take a lot back for a buyer on short notice."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"\"crews were sent to those twelve lots 140 times in the year before the gardens opened and 9 times in the year after\" (paragraph 2)"}]," ","— ","Strong evidence, but for a different point: it shows that gardens cut the city's cleanup costs. It says nothing about whether a lot can still be sold."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"\"new housing would bring in property taxes that a garden never will\" (paragraph 5)"}]," ","— ","This is part of the objection. It gives a reason to sell the lots, not a reason to believe a garden would allow a sale."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"\"When a builder bought one of the twelve lots last spring, the garden group harvested its last crop and cleared the lot before the deadline.\" (paragraph 5)"}]," ","— ","Correct. It shows the lease term working in practice: a lot was sold, and the gardeners left on time."]}],"$L45"]}]]]}]]}]
43:["$","details","ev-c4",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",4]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"Which detail supports a claim slightly different from the one the author uses it to support?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"D. The county survey of residents who live near a public park"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"Restate the claim in paragraph 4 first: gardens bring neighbors together. The survey is about parks, not gardens, so at most it supports a claim about parks — and even then it shows only that living near a park and trusting neighbors go together, not that one causes the other. The author closes the gap with an assumption, that a garden \"would do the same work,\" rather than with evidence."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"The pilot report's count of crew visits to the twelve lots before and after the gardens opened"}]," ","— ","This supports exactly the point it is used for — that gardens save the city money. It is relevant, measured, and sourced."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Loretta Banks's summer harvest from her pilot plot"}]," ","— ","Weak support, but for the right claim: it really is about a garden producing food. Its problem is sufficiency — one gardener cannot show what would happen across the city — not relevance."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"The builder who bought one of the pilot lots last spring"}]," ","— ","This supports exactly the point it is used for — that a garden does not block a sale."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"The county survey of residents who live near a public park"}]," ","— ","Correct. The survey measures something about parks, and the author stretches it to cover gardens. It supports a nearby claim, about parks, that the author did not make."]}]]}]]]}]]}]
44:["$","details","ev-c5",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",5]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"Is the author's evidence sufficient to support the central claim? In three or four sentences, name the strongest support in the passage, name one reason whose support falls short and explain what is wrong with it, and give your overall judgment."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-teal-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-teal-800","children":["Model response:"," "]}],"The strongest support is the pilot's first-year report, which found that crew visits to the twelve garden lots fell from 140 to 9 in a year, and the author also answers the developer objection with the sixty-day lease term and a lot that was actually sold. The other two reasons fall short: the food reason rests on one gardener's summer, and the neighbor reason rests on a survey about parks rather than gardens. Even the best evidence covers only twelve lots over a single year. The evidence is sufficient for a narrower claim, that gardens save the city money without blocking sales, but not for the author's promise that gardens would lower grocery bills and build trust across the city."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"A complete answer does three jobs: it credits the support that holds up, names the specific weakness in the support that does not — an anecdote standing in for evidence, or evidence about a different claim — and reaches a judgment that fits the evidence. The strongest answers notice that the evidence supports a narrower claim than the one the author makes. Judging the evidence insufficient is not the same as calling the claim false; the question is only whether the author supported it."]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]
45:["$","li","4",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["E","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"\"she has not bought a vegetable from June through September\" (paragraph 3)"}]," ","— ","One gardener's harvest, offered for the food reason in paragraph 3. It has nothing to do with selling a lot."]}]
